{
  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Log-Normal Distribution — Demo\n",
        "\n",
        "This notebook demonstrates the relationship between the **Normal Distribution** and **Log-Normal Distribution**.\n",
        "\n",
        "We will:\n",
        "- Generate Normally distributed data.\n",
        "- Apply the exponential transformation.\n",
        "- Observe the resulting Log-Normal distribution.\n",
        "- Apply a log transformation to recover an approximately Normal distribution.\n",
        "- Compare mean, median, and mode.\n",
        "- Use Q-Q plots as a diagnostic tool."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 1. Import Libraries"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
        "\n",
        "from scipy import stats"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 2. Generate a Normal Distribution\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Y \\sim N(2, 0.5^2)\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "We generate random observations from this distribution."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "np.random.seed(42)\n",
        "\n",
        "mu = 2\n",
        "sigma = 0.5\n",
        "n = 10000\n",
        "\n",
        "y = np.random.normal(mu, sigma, n)\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Mean of Y:\", np.mean(y))\n",
        "print(\"Standard deviation of Y:\", np.std(y))"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 3. Visualize the Normal Distribution"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "plt.figure(figsize=(10, 5))\n",
        "plt.hist(y, bins=50, density=True)\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"Y\")\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"Density\")\n",
        "plt.title(\"Normal Distribution\")\n",
        "plt.grid(True)\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 4. Transform Normal → Log-Normal\n",
        "\n",
        "Now apply the exponential transformation:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "X=e^Y\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The resulting variable $X$ follows a Log-Normal Distribution."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "x = np.exp(y)\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Minimum X:\", np.min(x))\n",
        "print(\"Maximum X:\", np.max(x))\n",
        "print(\"Mean X:\", np.mean(x))\n",
        "print(\"Median X:\", np.median(x))"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Notice that all values of $X$ are positive because:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "X=e^Y>0\n",
        "$$"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 5. Visualize the Log-Normal Distribution"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "plt.figure(figsize=(10, 5))\n",
        "plt.hist(x, bins=80, density=True)\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"X\")\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"Density\")\n",
        "plt.title(\"Log-Normal Distribution\")\n",
        "plt.grid(True)\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The distribution is **right-skewed** and has a long right tail."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 6. Compare Mean, Median and Mode\n",
        "\n",
        "For a Log-Normal Distribution:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\text{Mode}<\\text{Median}<\\text{Mean}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The mode can be estimated from the histogram."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "mean_x = np.mean(x)\n",
        "median_x = np.median(x)\n",
        "\n",
        "hist, bin_edges = np.histogram(x, bins=100)\n",
        "mode_x = (bin_edges[np.argmax(hist)] + bin_edges[np.argmax(hist) + 1]) / 2\n",
        "\n",
        "print(f\"Approximate Mode   : {mode_x:.4f}\")\n",
        "print(f\"Median             : {median_x:.4f}\")\n",
        "print(f\"Mean               : {mean_x:.4f}\")"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 7. Apply Log Transformation\n",
        "\n",
        "Now transform the Log-Normal variable back:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Y=\\ln(X)\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Since $X=e^Y$, taking the natural logarithm should recover the original approximately Normal data."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "log_x = np.log(x)\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Mean of log(X):\", np.mean(log_x))\n",
        "print(\"Standard deviation of log(X):\", np.std(log_x))"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 8. Visualize the Log-Transformed Data"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "plt.figure(figsize=(10, 5))\n",
        "plt.hist(log_x, bins=50, density=True)\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"ln(X)\")\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"Density\")\n",
        "plt.title(\"Log-Transformed Data\")\n",
        "plt.grid(True)\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The logarithmic transformation converts the strongly right-skewed Log-Normal data into data that is approximately **Normally distributed**."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 9. Q-Q Plot\n",
        "\n",
        "A Q-Q plot can be used to assess whether data is approximately Normally distributed.\n",
        "\n",
        "For a Log-Normal dataset, we should examine the Q-Q plot of **ln(X)** rather than only the original data."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "stats.probplot(log_x, dist=\"norm\", plot=plt)\n",
        "plt.title(\"Q-Q Plot of ln(X)\")\n",
        "plt.grid(True)\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "If the points approximately follow the reference line, the log-transformed data is reasonably consistent with a Normal Distribution.\n",
        "\n",
        "This is a **diagnostic**, not absolute proof of Normality."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 10. Theoretical Mean and Median\n",
        "\n",
        "For:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Y\\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "X=e^Y\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "the theoretical Log-Normal mean is:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "E[X]=e^{\\mu+\\frac{\\sigma^2}{2}}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and the median is:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{Median}(X)=e^\\mu\n",
        "$$"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "theoretical_mean = np.exp(mu + (sigma ** 2) / 2)\n",
        "theoretical_median = np.exp(mu)\n",
        "\n",
        "print(f\"Theoretical mean   : {theoretical_mean:.4f}\")\n",
        "print(f\"Sample mean        : {np.mean(x):.4f}\")\n        "print(f\"Theoretical median : {theoretical_median:.4f}\")\n",
        "print(f\"Sample median      : {np.median(x):.4f}\")"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Key Takeaways\n",
        "\n",
        "- A Log-Normal variable is obtained by exponentiating a Normal variable.\n",
        "- Taking `ln(X)` can transform Log-Normal data into approximately Normal data.\n",
        "- Log-Normal variables are strictly positive.\n",
        "- The distribution is generally right-skewed.\n",
        "- For a Log-Normal distribution, mean > median > mode.\n",
        "- Q-Q plots of the log-transformed data can help assess the Normality assumption."
      ]
    }
  ],
